    question: 'What is the statutory presumption of culpable mental state under Section 35 of the NDPS Act, 1985?',
    answer: 'Section 35 of the NDPS Act, 1985 creates a statutory reverse burden of proof regarding mens rea in NDPS prosecutions:\n\n1. **Sub-section (1)**: In any prosecution for an offence under this Act which requires a culpable mental state of the accused, the Court **shall presume the existence of such mental state**, but it shall be a defence for the accused to prove the fact that he had no such mental state with respect to the act charged as an offence in that prosecution.\n\n2. **Definition of Culpable Mental State (Explanation to Section 35)**: \"Culpable mental state\" includes intention, motive, knowledge of a fact, and belief in, or reason to believe, a fact.\n\n3. **Standard of Proof for Defence (Sub-section (2))**: A fact is said to be proved only when the court believes it to exist **beyond a reasonable doubt** and not merely when its existence is established by a preponderance of probability.\n\n*Judicial Prerequisite*: As held by the Supreme Court in Noor Aga v. State of Punjab (2008) and Hanif Khan v. Central Bureau of Narcotics (2020), the prosecution must first establish foundational facts (conscious possession or physical recovery) beyond reasonable doubt before the statutory presumption under Section 35 can be invoked against the accused.',
