    answer: 'Section 36 of the NDPS Act, 1985 empowers the Government to constitute Special Courts for speedy trial of offences:\n\n1. **Power to Constitute (Sub-section (1))**: The Government may, for the purpose of providing speedy trial of offences under this Act, by notification in the Official Gazette, constitute as many Special Courts as may be necessary for such area or areas as may be specified in the notification.\n\n2. **Composition and Qualifications (Sub-section (2) & (3))**:\n   * A Special Court shall consist of a single Judge who shall be appointed by the Government with the concurrence of the Chief Justice of the High Court.\n   * A person shall not be qualified for appointment as a Judge of a Special Court unless he was, immediately before such appointment, a **Sessions Judge or an Additional Sessions Judge**.\n\n3. **Jurisdiction**: Special Courts exercise all the powers of a Court of Session and can take cognizance of offences without the accused being committed to it for trial upon perusal of a police report or complaint filed by an authorized officer.',
