    question: 'Which offences are triable by Special Courts and what are the investigation timelines under Section 36A of the NDPS Act?',
    answer: 'Section 36A of the NDPS Act, 1985 defines the trial jurisdiction of Special Courts and modifies the standard Code of Criminal Procedure / BNSS investigation timelines:\n\n1. **Trial Jurisdiction (Section 36A(1)(a))**:\n   * All offences under the NDPS Act punishable with imprisonment for a term of **more than 3 years** shall be triable exclusively by the Special Court constituted for the area.\n   * Offences punishable with imprisonment up to 3 years may be tried summarily or by a Magistrate under Section 36A(5).\n\n2. **Direct Cognizance (Section 36A(1)(d))**:\n   * A Special Court may, upon perusal of police report or upon a complaint made by an officer of the Central Government or State Government authorized under this Act, take cognizance of that offence without the accused being committed to it for trial.\n\n3. **Investigation Timelines & Detention Period (Section 36A(4))**:\n   * For offences punishable under Section 19, Section 24, Section 27A, or offences involving commercial quantity, Section 167(2) of CrPC (Section 187 BNSS) is modified:\n   * The default period for completion of investigation and filing of chargesheet is **180 days** (instead of 90 days).\n   * If investigation cannot be completed within 180 days, the Special Court may extend the period **up to 1 year**, upon the report of the Public Prosecutor indicating the progress of the investigation and the specific reasons for the detention of the accused beyond 180 days.',
