    question: 'Who is empowered to issue search warrants and authorisations under Section 41 of the NDPS Act, 1985?',
    answer: 'Section 41 of the NDPS Act, 1985 distributes search and arrest authorization powers between judicial magistrates and empowered gazetted executive officers:\n\n1. **Magisterial Warrants (Section 41(1))**:\n   * A Metropolitan Magistrate, a Judicial Magistrate of the first class, or any Magistrate of the second class specially empowered by the State Government in this behalf, may issue a warrant for the arrest of any person whom he has reason to believe to have committed any offence punishable under Chapter IV, or for the search of any building, conveyance or place in which he has reason to believe any narcotic drug, psychotropic substance, controlled substance, or incriminating document/article is kept or concealed.\n\n2. **Authorisation by Gazetted Officers (Section 41(2))**:\n   * Any officer of gazetted rank of the Central Excise, Narcotics, Customs, Revenue Intelligence, or any other department of the Central Government, or of the Revenue, Drugs Control, Excise, Police, or other department of a State Government, empowered in this behalf by general or special order of the Central Government or State Government;\n   * If he has **reason to believe from personal knowledge or information given by any person and taken down in writing**;\n   * May authorize any officer subordinate to him but **superior in rank to a peon, sepoy, or a constable** to arrest such person or search a building, conveyance, or place.\n\n3. **Direct Exercise of Powers (Section 41(3))**:\n   * An officer to whom a warrant or authorisation is addressed under Section 41 may search, seize contraband, and arrest suspects with all the powers of an officer acting under Section 42.',
