    answer: 'Section 42 of the NDPS Act, 1985 governs warrantless search and seizure in private places/buildings and imposes strict procedural safeguards:\n\n1. **Empowered Officers (Section 42(1))**:\n   * Any officer (superior in rank to a peon, sepoy, or constable) of police, excise, narcotics, customs, revenue intelligence, or paramilitary forces empowered by general or special order.\n\n2. **Reason to Believe and Recording Information**:\n   * If the officer has reason to believe from personal knowledge or information taken down in writing that an NDPS offence has been committed;\n   * He may, between **sunrise and sunset**, enter into and search any building, conveyance, or enclosed place, break open locks if resisted, seize illicit substances, and arrest any offender.\n\n3. **Sunset to Sunrise Proviso (Night Searches)**:\n   * If the officer has reason to believe that a search warrant or authorisation cannot be obtained without affording opportunity for the concealment of evidence or facility for the escape of an offender, he may enter and search at any time between **sunset and sunrise**, **after recording the grounds of his belief**.\n\n4. **72-Hour Reporting Mandate (Section 42(2))**:\n   * Where an officer takes down any information in writing under sub-section (1) or records grounds for his belief under the proviso thereto, he shall **within seventy-two hours (72 hours)** send a copy thereof to his **immediate official superior**.\n\n*Judicial Precedents*: The Constitution Bench in Karnail Singh v. State of Haryana (2009) settled that compliance with Section 42(1) and 42(2) is mandatory, though substantial compliance in emergent situations is permissible if grounds are recorded and forwarded promptly without prejudice to the accused.',
