    answer: 'Section 43 of the NDPS Act, 1985 empowers officers to effect search, seizure, and arrest in public places and transit:\n\n1. **Power to Seize and Arrest**:\n   * Any officer of any of the departments mentioned in Section 42 may:\n     * Seize in any public place or in transit any narcotic drug or psychotropic substance or controlled substance in respect of which an offence has been committed, along with any animal or conveyance used in carrying it;\n     * Detain and search any person whom he has reason to believe to have committed an offence under Chapter IV, and arrest him and any other person in his company.\n\n2. **Definition of Public Place (Explanation to Section 43)**:\n   * \"Public place\" includes any public conveyance, hotel, shop, or any other place intended for use by, or accessible to, the public.\n\n3. **Critical Distinction between Section 42 and Section 43**:\n   * As held by the Supreme Court in State of Punjab v. Baldev Singh and Directorate of Revenue Intelligence v. Premlata (2024), **Section 43 does not require recording information in writing prior to search, nor does it require sending a copy to an official superior within 72 hours**.\n   * Section 42 applies to private buildings, conveyances, and enclosed private places; Section 43 applies to public spaces, open roads, and conveyances in transit in public areas.',
