    question: 'What are the mandatory conditions and legal safeguards for conducting personal search under Section 50 of the NDPS Act?',
    answer: 'Section 50 of the NDPS Act, 1985 provides mandatory statutory safeguards when searching a suspect\'s bodily person:\n\n1. **Option to be Searched Before Gazetted Officer or Magistrate (Section 50(1))**:\n   * When an empowered officer is about to search any person under Section 41, 42, or 43, he shall, **if such person so requires, take such person without unnecessary delay to the nearest Gazetted Officer of any of the departments mentioned in Section 42 or to the nearest Magistrate**.\n   * The suspect must be informed of this statutory right clearly (communicating this right in writing or orally with signed acknowledgement is standard practice).\n\n2. **Discretion of the Requisite Authority (Section 50(2) & (3))**:\n   * If requisitioned, the Gazetted Officer or Magistrate may discharge the person if no reasonable ground of suspicion is found, or direct that search be made.\n\n3. **Search of Female Persons (Section 50(4))**:\n   * **No female shall be searched by anyone excepting a female**.\n\n4. **Emergency Exception (Section 50(5) & (6))**:\n   * If it is not possible to take the person to a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate without the possibility of the person parting with possession of the contraband, the officer may proceed with the search, but must record reasons in writing and forward a copy to his immediate official superior within 72 hours.\n\n5. **Applicability Constraint (Physical Body Only)**:\n   * As settled by the Supreme Court Constitution Benches in State of Punjab v. Baldev Singh (1999) and Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja (2011), Section 50 applies **strictly and exclusively to personal searches of the human body and clothing worn**. It does NOT apply to searches of bags, luggage, containers, premises, or motor vehicles.',
