    answer: 'Section 52A of the NDPS Act, 1985 provides the statutory framework for pre-trial disposal of seized contraband and sampling under magisterial supervision:\n\n1. **Government Notification for Disposal (Section 52A(1))**:\n   * The Central Government may specify by notification the narcotic drugs, psychotropic substances, or conveyances which shall, having regard to hazardous nature, vulnerability to theft, or lack of storage space, be disposed of by an officer.\n\n2. **Inventory Preparation & Application to Magistrate (Section 52A(2))**:\n   * Where any contraband has been seized and forwarded under Section 52(3) or (4), the officer-in-charge shall prepare an inventory of such drugs containing details like description, quality, quantity, mark, numbers, country of origin, and packing;\n   * He shall make an application to any Magistrate for the purpose of:\n     * (a) **Certifying the correctness of the inventory** so prepared;\n     * (b) **Taking photographs** of such drugs or substances in the presence of the Magistrate and certifying such photographs as true; or\n     * (c) **Allowing him to draw representative samples of such drugs in the presence of such Magistrate and certifying the correctness of any list of samples so drawn**.\n\n3. **Primary Evidence Status (Section 52A(4))**:\n   * Notwithstanding anything contained in the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 (or Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023), every court trying an offence shall treat the inventory, the photographs of the drugs, and any list of samples certified by the Magistrate as **primary evidence** in respect of such offence.\n\n*Supreme Court Landmark Rulings*: In Union of India v. Mohan Lal (2016) and Mangilal v. State of MP (2023), the Supreme Court ruled that **samples MUST be drawn in the presence and under the certification of the Magistrate under Section 52A**. Samples drawn at the spot of seizure without Magisterial supervision do not constitute primary evidence.',
