    answer: 'Section 57 of the NDPS Act, 1985 mandates immediate supervisory reporting following an arrest or seizure:\n\n1. **Statutory Obligation**:\n   * Whenever any person makes any arrest or seizure under this Act, he shall, **within forty-eight hours (48 hours)** next after such arrest or seizure, make a full report of all the particulars of such arrest or seizure to his **immediate official superior**.\n\n2. **Distinction from Section 42(2) & Section 57A**:\n   * **Section 42(2)** requires recording information received or grounds for night search prior to search, and sending a copy to the superior within **72 hours**.\n   * **Section 57** requires a comprehensive post-operation report of the completed arrest and seizure within **48 hours**.\n   * **Section 57A** (under Chapter VA) requires reporting illegally acquired property identified during financial investigation to the designated authority within 48 hours.\n\n3. **Legal Status of Section 57**:\n   * The Supreme Court in *State of Punjab v. Balbir Singh (1994)* and *Gurbax Singh v. State of Haryana (2001)* held that Section 57 is directory in nature; however, investigating officers must not arbitrarily ignore it, as unexplained non-compliance may raise doubt about the bona fides of the seizure.',
