    answer: 'The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Rules, 1985 (framed under Section 9 and Section 76 of the NDPS Act, 1985) establish the comprehensive licensing and permit regime in India:\n\n1. **Opium Poppy Cultivation & Opium Production (Chapters III & IV)**:\n   * Opium poppy cultivation is strictly restricted to designated tracts in Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, and Uttar Pradesh under annual licences issued by the Central Bureau of Narcotics (CBN) under the Narcotics Commissioner of India.\n   * Cultivators must deliver all raw opium harvested to the Central Government Opium Factories (at Ghazipur and Neemuch).\n\n2. **Manufacture and Sale of Manufactured Drugs (Chapter V)**:\n   * Manufacture of manufactured drugs (morphine, codeine, thebaine) is prohibited except by licensed pharmaceutical manufacturers possessing quota allocations from the Narcotics Commissioner.\n\n3. **Import and Export Requirements (Chapter VI)**:\n   * International trade requires an official **Import Certificate** issued by the Narcotics Commissioner of India and a corresponding **Export Authorisation** issued by the exporting nation\'s competent authority in compliance with the 1961 Single Convention and 1971 Psychotropic Convention.\n\n4. **Controlled Substances Licencing**:\n   * Transport, consignment notes (Form 6 / consignor-consignee declaration), and consignment monitoring are enforced to prevent diversion into illicit channels.',
